In a related move, LCT promoted Robert Jones to Chief Brand and Strategy Officer, effective immediately. Both he and Weinstock will report to Managing Director Mike Schleifer and serve on LCT&#x2019;s executive team, alongside Kewsong Lee Artistic Director Lear deBessonet, Schleifer, Executive Producer Bartlett Sher, Stacey and Eric Mindich Producer Nicole Kastrinos, and Helen Gurley Brown Artistic Director of LCT3 and Producer Maria Manuela Goyanes.

&#x201C;We are thrilled to welcome Naomi to Lincoln Center Theater, and back to the Lincoln Center campus,&#x201D; said Lear deBessonet and Mike Schleifer in a statement. &#x201C;She brings an extraordinary depth of experience, a demonstrated ability to build and maintain meaningful relationships with supporters, and a track record of securing the kind of transformational philanthropic investment that allows an institution to think ambitiously about its future. She has a reputation as a thoughtful, respected leader, and we&#x2019;re very lucky to have her join the team.&#x201D;

&#x201C;I am honored to be joining Lear, Mike, and the executive team at Lincoln Center Theater, an organization I have long admired. There is an energy at the theater right now that is impossible to miss. I look forward to working with the Board, staff, and community of supporters to build upon an extraordinary legacy and help shape LCT&#x2019;s next great chapter,&#x201D; said Weinstock. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Jones, who joined LCT in the summer of 2025 as Chief Marketing Officer, assumes an expanded role as Chief Brand and Strategy Officer. In this position, he will lead overall identity, positioning, and reputation of the institution, with responsibility for the integrated strategy and execution of LCT&#x2019;s marketing, advertising, publicity, communications, partnerships, sales, customer experience, and merchandising. His expanded remit reflects the theater&#x2019;s commitment to building a distinctive institutional brand and deepening its relationship with audiences, while aligning the organization&#x2019;s mission and artistic vision with its business objectives and public identity.

&#x201C;We are thrilled to recognize Robert Jones with this expanded role at Lincoln Center Theater,&#x201D; said deBessonet and Schleifer. &#x201C;Our vision for the organization extends far beyond individual shows, centering how we serve our mission in the culture at large. Robert has distinguished himself as a remarkable leader in how we define ourselves as an institution, build relationships with our audiences, and shape our place in the cultural landscape. We look forward to this expanded role for an integral member of our team.&#x201D;

A few years ago, Iranian filmmaker Morteza Alizadeh saw a photograph that he couldn&#x2019;t shake from his mind: it showed mothers and their children going about their lives, which at first seemed routine, until the director realized it was taken inside a women&#x2019;s prison.

From that first glimpse into a little spoken-about reality, Alizadeh spent years researching how common it is for Iranian women to have children while waiting to be executed in prison. In &#x201C;Inherited,&#x201D; world premiering at the Cairo Film Festival , the director tells the fictional story of one of them, Mina (played by Mina Sadati). Sentenced to death for murder and running against the clock of her scheduled execution, she learns the only way to postpone her fate is to become pregnant. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories ‘How to Rob a Bank’ Review: Nicholas Hoult and Zoë Kravitz Take Their Paychecks in an Annoyingly Flashy, Emotionally Turgid Influencer-Age Heist Movie 'The Mongoose' Review: Liam Neeson Goes on the Run in a Bland Actioner

&#x201C;My main goal with this film was to portray people who find themselves in difficult and sensitive situations, where they are forced to make choices to survive,&#x201D; Alizadeh tells Variety . &#x201C;These choices affect not only their own lives but, more importantly, the lives of others.&#x201D;

The director dedicated a long period to research, collecting an extensive archive of images from inside prisons, which included rare documentaries and footage from hacked surveillance cameras. He also spent months meeting and interviewing women who had been imprisoned and had either been released temporarily or were on leave. &#x201C;With their help, we were able to design the entire prison environment in detail,&#x201D; he notes. &#x201C;Then our production design team recreated and built the whole prison set as closely as possible to the real thing.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

By posing a complex moral conundrum when Mina&#x2019;s family pleads with the unmarried prisoner to abort the baby to preserve the family&#x2019;s honor, the director wanted the audience to &#x201C;ask themselves&#x201D; how much right &#x201C;does one person have to decide another person's fate.&#x201D; &#x201C;What gives us the authority to decide whether to take someone's life or give another person the chance to live?&#x201D;

At the same time, it was important to me that the audience wouldn't simply judge the characters for their choices, but would also try to understand the circumstances and the structures that put them in such difficult situations,&#x201D; he adds.

The film&#x2019;s strong moral core and an unwillingness to offer easy answers speak to a long tradition of Iranian cinema established by names such as Jafar Panahi, Asghar Farhadi and Abbas Kiarostami. &#x201C;I believe the roots of this cinematic tradition, of creating morally complex situations, can be found in Iranian society and its social and cultural conditions,&#x201D; says Alizadeh.

In that sense, the director feels a &#x201C;deep connection&#x201D; between &#x201C;Inherited&#x201D; and the work of his countrymen, which all come from a &#x201C;shared reality of Iranian society&#x201D; and are &#x201C;influenced by the circumstances we live in.&#x201D; &#x201C;The experiences, contradictions and complexities of our society gradually build up inside us and find their way into our films, consciously or unconsciously.&#x201D;

Speaking of his home country, Alizadeh says the situation in Iran has become &#x201C;increasingly complex.&#x201D; &#x201C;Alongside the difficult economic conditions that have created countless problems for people, to the point where simply getting through everyday life has become almost impossible for the majority, two wars in less than a year, people's resistance against these attacks, and public protests between the two wars have made the situation in the country even more complicated.&#x201D;

&#x201C;As a result,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;It has become harder for artists to understand and respond to the social conditions around them.&#x201D;

This week, major industry names such as Mark Ruffalo and Tilda Swinton signed a statement shared with Variety that says they are &#x201C;deeply concerned&#x201D; by reports of the new prison sentence of five years and one month issued by the Tehran Revolutionary Court against Panahi, as well as the confiscation of his assets, the freezing of his bank accounts, a two-year travel ban and a two-year social media ban. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Iranian cinema is going through a very difficult period as it tries to redefine its relationship with these circumstances and overcome both internal obstacles, such as the relationship between filmmakers and society, and external ones, particularly censorship,&#x201D; he goes on. &#x201C;This makes it harder and slower for new talents to emerge. I believe the most important thing that can help move Iranian cinema forward and create opportunities for new filmmakers is their courage to tell stories while staying true to their own personal voice and style.&#x201D;

For Iraqi-Swiss filmmaker Ali Toffan Al-Fatlawi , the idea behind &#x201C;The Blind Ferryman&#x201D; began with a thought that struck him during Iraq&#x2019;s civil war in 2006. Living in Switzerland and watching the violence unfold in his homeland, he thought of two of his aunts who were blind and found himself wishing that blindness might spare his countrymen from the violence they were inflicting upon one another.

That idea became the starting point for his fiction feature debut, which explores what it means to see, believe and trust our own perceptions. The film had its world premiere at the 47th Cairo Intl. Film Festival. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;I think we lost the ability to think with our feelings,&#x201D; Al-Fatlawi tells Variety. &#x201C;Or, as we say in Arabic, &#x2018;Seeing things with our heart.&#x2019;&#x201D;

Set in Iraq&#x2019;s southern marshlands, the film follows Ayoub (Ahmed Moneka), a blind boatman who falls in love with Genya (Sam Rahmani), a mysterious woman whom nobody else can see. Convinced she exists only in his imagination, his community mocks him, forcing Ayoub to defend a love in which only he believes.

&#x201C;When you love someone, why do you need the agreement of other people?&#x201D; Al-Fatlawi asks. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s your love, it&#x2019;s your own decision.&#x201D;

The character of Genya draws on stories Al-Fatlawi heard from his father about a mythical woman who appeared to men in the marshlands, as well as ancient Sumerian mythology and the &#x201C;Epic of Gilgamesh.&#x201D; The filmmaker was particularly fascinated by the prominence of female deities in ancient Mesopotamian mythology, questioning how those figures gave way to the predominantly male image of God that characterizes contemporary religion. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The marshlands themselves were equally important to his vision. Beyond their mythical associations, they offered Al-Fatlawi an opportunity to portray a side of Iraq rarely seen on screen, challenging the familiar images of war, poverty and destruction that have long dominated international representations of the country.

&#x201C;I don&#x2019;t want to do a movie which shows that we are poor people, asking for sympathy,&#x201D; he insists. &#x201C;I&#x2019;m really very careful not to make a movie which makes the West feel good about their life.&#x201D;

His determination to challenge preconceived notions also informed his casting. Ayoub is a Black Iraqi, while the threatening outsider who arrives from the city is a white, blue-eyed man dressed in religious clothing, a deliberate challenge to stereotypes about who looks like a hero and who looks like a villain.

That tension between appearance and reality runs throughout the film, shaping everything from its characters to its visual language. A longtime painter and performance artist who later expanded into filmmaking, Al-Fatlawi developed the screenplay through drawings, often painting scenes before putting them into words.

&#x201C;I paint and then I write, I paint and then I write. That&#x2019;s my way to write,&#x201D; he explains.

Working with his cinematographer, he chose the 4:3 aspect ratio to bring characters closer together within the frame, while composing wide shots that divide the image equally between the landscape and its reflection in the water.

&#x201C;I told him, I want really in the middle, 50%,&#x201D; he recalls. &#x201C;There is the image, and the reflection of the image in the water.&#x201D;

That same attention to perception shaped his direction of the actors. Al-Fatlawi instructed Moneka to keep his eyes open and fixed throughout the film, resisting conventional portrayals of blindness that might invite pity.

&#x201C;I don&#x2019;t want people to feel sorry for you,&#x201D; he recalls telling the actor. &#x201C;I want you to be strong. You see more than everybody who has eyes.&#x201D;

For Rahmani, the challenge was to embody Genya as both a flesh-and-blood woman and a potentially mythical presence. Al-Fatlawi asked her to play the character as 50% real and 50% imaginary, a delicate balance they worked through in rehearsals and during filming. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;I had to portray the whole spectrum without losing credibility as either,&#x201D; Rahmani tells Variety. &#x201C;Sometimes I went against my instinct, but that&#x2019;s why I give so much credit to Ali as the creator of this whole world.&#x201D;

The experience was also a personal homecoming for the actress, who grew up in Jordan and Canada and had never lived in Iraq. After years of speaking Iraqi Arabic almost exclusively at home, spending nearly four months in the marshlands gave her the chance to use her native dialect freely, even in banal everyday encounters at supermarkets and restaurants. Rahmani says her time there brought her closer to her Iraqi heritage.

&#x201C;It was such a surreal and, I don&#x2019;t know, special feeling full of relief,&#x201D; she recalls. &#x201C;I can only describe it as not having to mask all the time and code switch. I could just be myself.&#x201D;

&#x201C;The Blind Ferryman gave me that gift, Ali gave me that gift of being more comfortable in my own skin,&#x201D; she says. &#x201C;It was healing for me.&#x201D;

The project began development in 2015, receiving support from the Doha Film Institute, El Gouna and Red Sea film festivals and participating in Cannes&#x2019; L&#x2019;Atelier program in 2022. That same year, it secured approximately $2.5 million in financing.

After a 45-day shoot in Iraq, however, Al-Fatlawi still had one crucial sequence to complete: an underwater scene he had envisioned years earlier.

Rather than abandon it or spend an estimated $100,000 to $150,000 on a water tank, he found a cenote near Playa del Carmen, Mexico, whose submerged vegetation bore a striking resemblance to a painting he had made in 2021. Working with two underwater photographers who had never shot a feature film, he completed the sequence for a relatively modest $15,000.

At CIFF, Al-Fatlawi says he has been particularly moved by audiences discovering meanings in the film that he had never consciously intended.

&#x201C;They add things which make the movie even richer,&#x201D; he reflects. &#x201C;They have the doors open. They can enter from everywhere.&#x201D;

Kate Middleton is starring in her first documentary, and it's a personal one. Heal , which will premiere on the BBC in the UK on November 29 and NBC in the U.S. on November 30, follows the Princess of Wales as she undertakes the National Three Peaks Challenge and reflects on her life as a cancer survivor.

In June, Middleton completed the National Three Peaks Challenge, climbing the highest mountains in Scotland, England and Wales within 24 hours to support The Royal Marsden Cancer Charity. She had a crew filming her for the documentary, the existence of which had not been revealed until now. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

