For Egyptian filmmaker Sara Shazli , the camera became a way of asking her mother questions she had carried since childhood. Returning to Cairo from film school in Cuba, she would wait in the family kitchen, camera in hand, hoping to understand the woman whose devotion to cinema had so often left her feeling alone.

&#x201C;Was the camera protecting me from the answers she could give me? Was the camera a way of having her attention?,&#x201D; Shazli wonders. With cinema occupying such a central place in her mother&#x2019;s life, filming their conversations offered a way of reaching her through the very medium that had so often kept them apart. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories How Soviet TV Audiences Fell Under the Spell of Mass Hypnosis Artist Anatoly Kashpirovskiy Ji.hlava New Visions Award Winner Karim Akalay Mixes Magic With ‘Anti-Social Realism’ in ‘6TM’: ‘It’s About Collective Strength Rather Than One Hero’

Those encounters became the foundation of &#x201C; Your Daughter ,&#x201D; Shazli&#x2019;s documentary about her relationship with her mother Marianne Khoury , the Egyptian producer and filmmaker who spent decades working alongside her uncle, renowned director Youssef Chahine. Premiering at the 47th Cairo Intl. Film Festival, the film charts a daughter&#x2019;s changing understanding of her mother, from resentment over her childhood absence to an admiration she had never anticipated.

Shazli initially conceived the project as a search for Woody, the nanny who had helped raise her, hoping to reconnect with a figure who had played such an important role in her childhood. But as she developed the film and attended workshops with other filmmakers, their observations helped her recognize a more deeply personal story taking shape in the footage of her mother.

&#x201C;Someone told me, &#x2018;Look at how you film your mother. You film her with so much love,&#x2019;&#x201D; she recalls. &#x201C;It was actually painful,&#x201D; she adds, because acknowledging that love meant confronting difficult questions, ones she had avoided for far too long. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Those questions extended beyond Khoury&#x2019;s absence to the expectations she passed on to her daughter. &#x201C;I grew up with a mother telling me that you have to be independent from any man in the world,&#x201D; Shazli says. &#x201C;But it was so extreme. Why can&#x2019;t both the dad and the mom help? Why can&#x2019;t both be responsible?&#x201D;

